'''Zialo''' ([[self-identification]] ''Ziolo'') is a language spoken by the [[Zialo people]] in [[Guinea]].

The language of Zialo, which belongs to the Southwestern group of the [[Mande languages|Mande]] branch of the [[Niger–Congo languages|Niger–Congo]] language family, is spoken by approximately 25,000 people residing in the province of [[Macenta]] in the southeast of Guinea. The Zialo area covers over 50 villages (including two centers of subprefectures). Nearly a third of all Zialo live now in the nearby towns of [[Macenta]] and [[Guéckédou]], as well as in the city of [[Conakry]]. The Zialo language does not have its own writing system; people use French in all official paperwork.

Zialo was recognized as a distinct language and studied by the Moscow-based linguist {{ill|Kirill Babaev|ru|Бабаев, Кирилл Владимирович}}, member of the [http://mandelang.kunstkamera.ru/index/mandelang/rles_westafr1/ Russian Linguistic Expedition] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100505054353/http://mandelang.kunstkamera.ru/index/mandelang/rles_westafr1/ |date=2010-05-05 }} to Guinea, in January–February 2010. Before that, Zialo was considered a remote dialect of the language of [[Loma people|Loma]]; however, significant differences were discovered between the two. The tonology and phonetics of Zialo do resemble Loma, but the lexicon and the morphological systems of Zialo are closer to those of [[Bandi language|Bandi]] and [[Mende language|Mende]]. Zialo is characterized by the extensive use of nasal vowels and consonants, the wide system of initial consonant alternations, a great number of analytic verbal constructions of time and aspect, and over 15 sets of personal pronouns. Zialo speakers name five major dialects of the language: Bayawa, Wolo-Ziolo, Woyjawa, Kelighigo and Lawolozu, of which the last one seems the most specific.

The Zialo probably came to their present location from the south, the present-day territory of [[Liberia]], as indicated by their legends. Nowadays, the majority of Zialo are Christians; there are also groups of Muslims and animists.

